 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Voxel read and write access operates in constant time $O(k)$, though with a larger constant factor $k$ than an ordinary dense buffer due to the overhead of locating the relevant data [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of active cells, as only explicitly added cells allocate memory [S1, unofficial].
-- **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-- **Scaling:** Scales linearly with the number of active cells; performance is independent of the total volume size defined by `shape` [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Efficient for large domains with small active regions; explicit lattice structure allows easy coordinate conversion; supports dynamic add/remove operations [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** No inherent spatial locality for traversal compared to tree structures; hash/set overhead for index management [G].
-- **When to use:** Large sparse volumes, dynamic updates, explicit lattice requirements [S1, unofficial].
-- **When not to use:** Dense volumes, hierarchical traversal needed, multi-resolution requirements [G].
+- **Time Complexity**: 
+  - Add/Remove: O(1) amortized for vector append, O(N) for search if not using a hash set [G].
+  - Get Points/Values: O(N) where N is the number of active cells [S1, unofficial].
+  - Filter: O(N) [S1, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity**: O(N) where N is the number of active cells. Each cell stores 8 floats and 1 integer index [S1, unofficial].
+- **Measured Performance**: Not given in the sources.
+- **Scaling**: Scales linearly with the number of active cells. Independent of the total volume size [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths**: Simple implementation, low memory overhead for sparse data, easy to integrate with GPU pipelines [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations**: No spatial coherence exploitation, poor cache locality for random access, no built-in LOD [G].
+- **When to Use**: Small iso-surface regions in large domains, simple adaptive grids [S1, unofficial].
+- **When Not to Use**: Dense volumes, need for hierarchical LOD, complex spatial queries [G].
 
 ## Background
-SparseGrid is described in the context of GPU-based volume rendering and iso-surface extraction, specifically within the `isoext` project [S1, unofficial]. It is designed to work with marching tetrahedra and dual mesh generation algorithms [S1, unofficial]. The structure inherits from a base `Grid` class and stores data on the device using Thrust [S1, unofficial].
+SparseGrid is part of the `isoext` library by Guangyan Cai, used for GPU-accelerated iso-surface extraction [S1, unofficial]. It is designed to work with marching tetrahedra and dual mesh generation, providing a flexible sparse data structure for active cells [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Sources
